        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The denominator is $0$.",
          "worked": "At $x=1$, the numerator is $1^2-1=0$ and the denominator is $1-1=0$. The quotient is undefined at $x=1$, so direct substitution does not determine the limit.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p5-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By first simplifying $\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-1}$ before substituting in $x=1$, evaluate the same limit.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2$\n\nFactorise the numerator and cancel the common factor before substituting.",
          "worked": "For $x\\ne1$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}&=\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}\\\\\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}&=x+1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ in the simplified expression.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}&=\\lim_{x\\to1}(x+1)\\\\\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}&=1+1\\\\\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}&=2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      }
    ]
  },
